      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It is a fair critique that the tangible benefits of actual safety often feel more urgent than abstract theoretical fears. However, proponents of the pro-privacy side argue that \"slippery slopes\" aren't just vibes; they are real historical precedents where minor privacy encroachments eventually normalized into pervasive state control. The core disagreement is whether we prioritize absolute safety at the cost of civil liberties, or if the first citizen must unilaterally assert that one \"vague fear\" could justify a complete erosion of privacy rights that no one can ever opt out of.",
